All of the xar, bill-of-materials, cpio, flat-package, signing, notarization
and stapling code in this repository is original. The formats it implements
are Apple's, and understanding them required reading other people's
implementations. This file records what was read and what was taken from it,
so the provenance of every package is stated rather than guessed. Nothing
below is imported or vendored; each entry is a reference that informed a
design, and none of its expression was copied, with one stated exception:
entry 14, whose C is translated into Go and whose license travels with it.

1. xar (Apple): https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xar
   Copyright (c) 2005-2007 Rob Braun; BSD-3-Clause.
   Authoritative header layout, checksum algorithm numbering (3 = SHA-256,
   4 = SHA-512 in Apple's fork), table-of-contents element set, timestamp
   formats and the way `xar --sign` reserves signature space. Informed
   pkg/xar.

2. xar (mackyle fork): https://github.com/mackyle/xar
   BSD-3-Clause. Documents the upstream divergence in which checksum
   algorithm 3 means "other" with an extended header, and the
   application/zlib encoding alias. pkg/xar reads both conventions.

3. libarchive: https://github.com/libarchive/libarchive
   Copyright (c) 2010-2012 Michihiro NAKAJIMA; BSD-2-Clause.
   The only complete standalone C xar writer. Its TOC construction order,
   <data> field order, encoding MIME strings and reserved-digest technique
   were cross-checked against Apple's writer. Informed pkg/xar's writer.

4. 7-Zip: XarHandler.cpp. LGPL-2.1. Read as a reference for which
   malformed inputs a practical reader tolerates. No code taken.

5. Kaitai Struct xar.ksy: https://github.com/kaitai-io/kaitai_struct_formats
   CC0-1.0. Formal header specification.

6. relic: https://github.com/sassoftware/relic
   Copyright (c) SAS Institute Inc.; Apache-2.0.
   The design foundation for pkg/pkgsign: the RSA-plus-CMS heap layout, the
   signature-space reservation and the strip-and-shift re-signing approach.
   Re-implemented; relic is not a dependency of this project.

7. apple-platform-rs: https://github.com/indygreg/apple-platform-rs
   Copyright (c) Gregory Szorc. apple-bom is MIT OR Apache-2.0; apple-xar,
   apple-flat-package, cpio-archive and apple-codesign are MPL-2.0.
   The best prose documentation of the flat package, bill of materials,
   cpio and xar signing formats, and of the notarization ticket trailer.
   Read for the formats only; no MPL-licensed code was transliterated.

8. bomutils: https://github.com/hogliux/bomutils
   Copyright (c) 2013 Fabian Renn; GPL-2.0.
   Read only, for the bill-of-materials structure and the tutorial on
   assembling a flat package without Apple's tools. No code was taken, so
   that pkg/bom can be MIT.

9. ipsw: https://github.com/blacktop/ipsw
   Copyright (c) blacktop; MIT.
   Its pkg/bom reader and pkg/ota/pbzx reader informed pkg/bom's reader and
   pkg/pbzx.

10. munki-pkg: https://github.com/munki/munki-pkg
    Copyright (c) Greg Neagle; Apache-2.0.
    The build-info manifest schema that `macospkg build --manifest` accepts
    is compatible with it by design.

11. lzfse (go-compressions): https://github.com/go-compressions/lzfse
    Copyright (c) the go-compressions authors; MIT (a Go port of Apple's
    LZFSE, https://github.com/lzfse/lzfse, BSD-3-Clause).
    Imported as a dependency: the LZFSE codec behind pbze containers and
    Apple Archive.

12. lz4 (pierrec): https://github.com/pierrec/lz4
    Copyright (c) 2015 Pierre Curto; BSD-3-Clause.
    Imported as a dependency: the LZ4 block codec inside Apple's bv41
    frames (pbz4 containers and Apple Archive).

13. xnu: https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu
    Copyright (c) Apple Inc.; APSL-2.0.
    osfmk/vm/lz4.h documents Apple's LZ4 frame tags (bv41, bv4-, bv4$) and
    bsd/vfs/vfs_xattr.c the AppleDouble ATTR layout. Read for the formats;
    no code taken.

14. libzbitmap: https://github.com/eafer/libzbitmap
    Copyright (c) 2022 Corellium LLC; author Ernesto A. Fernandez; MIT.
    Apple publishes no specification for LZBITMAP, and this is the only
    implementation of it: the format was reverse-engineered there by black
    box testing. pkg/lzbitmap is a translation of that C into Go, not a
    reference that informed our own design, so the MIT license above
    applies to it and this notice carries the copyright. The two are kept
    honest by Apple's own aa: pkg/lzbitmap decodes what aa writes, and aa
    extracts what pkg/lzbitmap writes.

Third-party Go modules this project depends on are listed in go.mod; each
carries its own license. Apple's root certificates in pkg/pkgsign/roots
were exported from macOS's system root store and are Apple's.
